\ A locked binary's dispatch-miss trap leaves the stack the interpreter leaves, so the restore
\ before the handler releases every slot. The handler sees both restored operands above the
\ sentinel. The catch sits in a word with declared outputs, so the comparisons after it see two
\ values rather than an opaque row.

check: ( ok label -- ) [ swap [ "PASS: " ] [ "FAIL: " ] if swap #append print-line ] ;

add-any: ( a b -- c ) [ + ] ;

catch-add: ( -- sentinel caught ) [ 5 "a" 1 [ add-any ] [ drop nip 1 = ] recover ] ;

catch-add "+ of a string is caught and the handler sees the restored operand" check
5 = "+ of a string leaves the sentinel" check
